# Rezztor EAgent configuration. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
# INSTALL.md step 4 generates all of this for you; this file is here so you
# can see what the deployment expects without reading the Compose file.

# Which release to run. Pin a date rather than tracking `latest`, so an
# upgrade is a decision you make rather than something a restart does.
REZZTOR_VERSION=2026.09.08

# The port the interface is served on. 50080 unless something else has it.
REZZTOR_PORT=50080

# Optional: the host address that port is published on. Leave it out to publish
# on every address. Set 127.0.0.1 when a TLS proxy on this host is the only
# thing that should reach it.
# REZZTOR_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# Optional: the container networks. All seven sit in <prefix>.248.0/21, by
# default 172.31.248.0/21. If your network uses that range, set two other
# numbers here BEFORE the first start (10.99 gives 10.99.248.0/21). The
# installer checks the block against this host's addresses and routes.
# REZZTOR_NET_PREFIX=172.31

# Optional: a proxy for outbound traffic. The application, the cloud executor
# and the runner get these; the Ansible executor gets none. The names the stack
# resolves by itself are always exempt, and NO_PROXY adds yours. A proxy on a
# private address must also be listed in /etc/rezztor/guard.json (INSTALL.md,
# step 6), or the guard keeps the runner away from it.
# HTTP_PROXY=http://proxy.example.com:3128
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.example.com:3128
# NO_PROXY=.example.com,10.0.0.0/8

# Seed administrator, applied on first boot only. Change the password in the
# interface once you are in; after that this value does nothing.
# Put a password you type in single quotes: unquoted, Compose reads `$word` as
# a variable and cuts the value at ` #`.
REZZTOR_ADMIN_USER=admin
REZZTOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Signs session cookies. Must stay the same forever -- change it and every
# user is signed out on the next restart. Generate once:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=

# Public half of the key the application uses to reach the runner. Generate
# the pair with `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ./runner_key -N ""`, then paste the
# contents of runner_key.pub here. The private half stays in this directory
# and is mounted read-only; the runner never sees it.
RUNNER_AUTHORIZED_KEY=

# --- Network automation (optional) ------------------------------------------
# The Ansible executor reaches switches, routers and firewalls. It runs when
# COMPOSE_PROFILES names it (COMPOSE_PROFILES=ansible, or ansible,cloud), and
# not otherwise. An installer that was asked for it writes the three secrets
# below; to switch it on later, add them once by hand. Leave them blank
# without the executor: a job token alone switches the plugin on.
#
# The token the application authenticates to the executor with. Without one the
# executor refuses every request rather than accepting any. Generate once:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_EXECUTOR_TOKEN=

# A second token, for editing the executor's inventory and device credentials.
# It must not be the same value as the one above: the agent carries that one,
# and a shared value would let a compromised application rewrite the inventory
# its own playbooks reach. The executor refuses the configuration plane outright
# if the two match. Generate separately:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# The application is given that second token too, so Settings -> Network
# automation edits the inventory and the credentials with nothing to paste. To
# keep it away from the application, uncomment the next line and leave it
# empty: the application then runs the reviewed playbooks and cannot change
# which devices they reach, and the token is pasted into Settings only by
# whoever decides to.
# REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_APP_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Encrypts the device credentials the executor stores. 32 bytes, base64:
#   head -c32 /dev/urandom | base64
# Without it the executor stores no credential at all, rather than storing one
# in the clear. Keep a copy: changing it makes every stored credential
# undecryptable, and the only way back is entering them again.
REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_CREDENTIAL_KEY=

# The application reaches the executor over a Unix socket in a volume only those
# two containers mount (ADR 0051). There is no TCP address to set instead: the
# executor listens on no port, and the application is not on its network.
#
# Each of the three secrets above may instead be a file, so the value is not in
# this .env: REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_CREDENTIAL_KEY_FILE, REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_EXECUTOR_TOKEN_FILE
# and REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_ADMIN_TOKEN_FILE name a path inside the executor container
# (a Docker secret under /run/secrets/). Setting both forms of one is refused.
# See docs/guides/security-credentials.md.

# The executor's break-glass console: a read-only page showing what has run, for
# when the application itself is the thing that is broken.
#
# Leave it blank and there is no listener at all. Set a port (8600, say) and it
# binds inside the container only -- the image has Python but no curl, so reach
# it with:
#
#   docker compose exec ansible python -c "
#   import base64, urllib.request
#   t='$REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_ADMIN_TOKEN'
#   r=urllib.request.Request('http://127.0.0.1:8600/')
#   r.add_header('Authorization','Basic '+base64.b64encode(f'x:{t}'.encode()).decode())
#   print(urllib.request.urlopen(r).read().decode()[:2000])"
#
# or by forwarding it. Publishing it to the host means adding a `ports:` entry to
# docker-compose.yml yourself, and that needs TLS in front of it: the console
# authenticates with HTTP Basic, so the token travels in the clear.
REZZTOR_ANSIBLE_PUBLIC_PORT=

# --- Database (required) -----------------------------------------------------
# Everything durable except uploads and editable content lives in PostgreSQL.
# There is no file fallback; a deployment without these does not start.
#
# Two roles, deliberately. REZZTOR_DB_PASSWORD owns the data and is what
# migrations run as. REZZTOR_DB_APP_PASSWORD is the role the application serves
# requests as, and it cannot bypass row-level security -- which is what keeps
# one user's conversations out of another's, now that a directory no longer
# does it.
#
# The installer generates both. Do not change them by hand once installed: the
# first is what the data volume was initialised with, and changing it locks the
# Web search runs in its own container and signs its cookies with this. It
# refuses to start without one rather than using a shipped default. Generate
# with: openssl rand -hex 32
REZZTOR_SEARXNG_SECRET=

# deployment out of its own accounts and conversations.
REZZTOR_DB_PASSWORD=
REZZTOR_DB_APP_PASSWORD=

# --- Azure changes (optional) -----------------------------------------------
# Only needed if you run the cloud executor (`docker compose --profile cloud up
# -d`), which makes changes in Azure after an engineer enters a CR or SR number.
# It never deletes, stops, restarts or resizes anything. The installer generates
# all five; an installation that predates them appends them once (INSTALL.md,
# "Optional: Azure changes"). Never replace this file.
#
# The two tokens must differ: the executor refuses its administration plane if
# they match. Generate each with:  openssl rand -hex 32
REZZTOR_CLOUD_JOB_TOKEN=
REZZTOR_CLOUD_ADMIN_TOKEN=
# Seals the Azure keys at rest. 32 bytes, base64:  openssl rand -base64 32
REZZTOR_CLOUD_KEY=
# Encrypts OpenTofu's state and plan files, at least 16 characters:  openssl rand -hex 32
# Keep a copy beside this file: the database holds the state only as ciphertext,
# and without this nothing can open it.
REZZTOR_CLOUD_STATE_PASSPHRASE=
# The database role the executor connects as (eagent_cloud). The application
# creates it from this at startup; it can read OpenTofu's state and write its own
# record of what it applied, and nothing else. Generate:  openssl rand -hex 24
REZZTOR_DB_CLOUD_PASSWORD=
# Each of the secrets above may instead be a file (a Docker secret), so the value
# is not in this .env: add _FILE to its name. Setting both forms of one is refused.
